.TH CYAML_NODE_SET_TAG 3 "2026-08-17" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
cyaml_node_set_tag \- attach, replace, or clear a node's YAML tag
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cyaml.h>

ccol_retval_t cyaml_node_set_tag(cyaml node, const char *tag);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Attach a tag to node, replace the tag of node, or clear it.  The node
makes its own copy of tag.

This function sets metadata only; it never changes the type or the
value that node already has.

A core-schema tag names exactly one node type:

  CYAML_TAG_NULL   -> CYAML_NULL
  CYAML_TAG_BOOL   -> CYAML_BOOL
  CYAML_TAG_INT    -> CYAML_INTEGER
  CYAML_TAG_FLOAT  -> CYAML_FLOAT
  CYAML_TAG_STR    -> CYAML_STRING
  CYAML_TAG_SEQ    -> CYAML_LIST
  CYAML_TAG_MAP    -> CYAML_DICTIONARY

The function refuses a core-schema tag on a node of any other type
with ccol_invalid_args and leaves the node untouched, because
cyaml_parse(3) would refuse such a pairing or read it back as a
different type.  A caller that wants a string node must construct one
with cyaml_create_string(3) and then tag it.  A custom tag, which is
any tag other than those seven, is valid on a node of any type.

A custom tag that you set here survives a later cyaml_set(3) call on
the same node, while a core-schema tag survives such a call only while
it still names the type of the node: cyaml_set(3) drops a core-schema
tag that the new value no longer matches.  cyaml_serialize(3) and
cyaml_serialize_flow(3) write a custom tag in its fully resolved
verbatim form and omit a core-schema tag, because implicit typing
restores the type of its node with no help.

A tag must be well-formed UTF-8: the function refuses one that is not
with ccol_invalid_args and leaves the tag that node already has
untouched.  It accepts any other string that is not empty as a tag,
including a string that holds a character which the URI characters of
YAML 1.2 do not allow literally in the serialized output, such as a
\&'>', a line break, a '%', a space, a control character or any
non-ASCII character.  The serializer writes each byte of such a
character as a percent escape, so a second parse of the tag gives
exactly the string that you set here.

The empty string is not a valid tag: the function rejects it with
ccol_invalid_args and leaves the tag that node already has untouched.
Because the serializer writes a custom tag in its verbatim "!<...>"
form and cyaml_parse(3) does not accept "!<>", an empty tag would give
a document that this library cannot parse again.  Pass NULL to clear a
tag.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
node  Target node of any type.  For NULL, the function reports
      ccol_invalid_args and changes nothing.
tag   New tag string, which the node copies.  It must be well-formed
      UTF-8 and must not be the empty string.  NULL clears the tag that
      the node has.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success.  The function returns ccol_invalid_args when node is
NULL, when tag is the empty string, when tag is not well-formed UTF-8,
or when tag is a core-schema tag that does not name the type of node,
and ccol_not_enough_memory on an allocation failure.  On every failure
it leaves the tag that node already has untouched.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cyaml_node_tag (3),
.BR cyaml_set (3),
.BR cyaml_create_string (3)
